[SIZE=2][I]Part 3 of 3. Sections: 5. Performance, 6. Conclusion[/I][/SIZE]

[SIZE=5][B]5. Performance[/B][/SIZE]

The unit of measure is variable operations ([ICODE]set_variable[/ICODE] / [ICODE]change_variable[/ICODE]), the dominant cost in Jomini script.

[B]5.1 Unit costs[/B]

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Stage[/TH][TH]Cost[/TH][TH]Detail[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]raw stage, per location[/TD][TD]70[/TD][TD]five strata's income, spending, savings pressure, raw coefficient[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]classify + matrix aggregation, per location[/TD][TD]37[/TD][TD]share scoring, clipping, confidence, column accumulation[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]solver setup, per country[/TD][TD]120[/TD][TD]variable resets, target and baseline sums, capacity split[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]exact solve (4x4)[/TD][TD]112[/TD][TD]Gaussian elimination + back substitution + nonnegativity check[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]one vertex case[/TD][TD]218[/TD][TD]elimination + back substitution + two metrics + accept test[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]full vertex sweep (209 cases)[/TD][TD]45,562[/TD][TD]previous row x 209[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

One sweep costs 45,562 against the exact solve's 112 — a factor of 407. So the performance bottleneck is simply how many countries fall through into the sweep (the approximate path).

Recall the data from 4.1: nearly all countries that enter the solver do. That is precisely why the prefilter exists.

[B]5.2 Monthly totals[/B]

The figures below use section 4's measured path distribution — sweep counts are the countries that actually reach a sweep, not an estimate from the feasibility upper bound. Listed per timepoint:

[TABLE]
[TR][TH]Configuration[/TH][TH]1337.10[/TH][TH]1386.03[/TH][TH]1743.04[/TH][/TR]
[TR][TD]all on + prefilter[/TD][TD]22,520,315[/TD][TD]19,498,487[/TD][TD]16,635,780[/TD][/TR]
[TR][TD]default (prefilter + AI reduced frequency)[/TD][TD]2,744,997 (12.2%)[/TD][TD]2,507,632 (12.9%)[/TD][TD]2,663,573 (16.0%)[/TD][/TR]
[/TABLE]

The prefilter's benefit was given in 4.3: it trades a small amount of traversal for most of the sweep triggers.

AI frequency reduction saves a further 84% to 88%. In single player there is one human country and the rest are AI, whose solving moves to their own anniversary pulse, so only about 1/12 of them solve in any given month. The raw stage and the modifier writes are not reduced — they run every month regardless — so what is saved is the solving portion, and that portion was over 90% of the total to begin with.

1743's reduction benefit is slightly lower (84.0% versus 87.8%) because it has the most locations, and since the raw stage is unaffected by frequency reduction it takes a larger share of the total.

Worth noting that the three timepoints' totals are fairly close (16.6M to 22.5M) even though the country count falls from 1,441 to 589. The reason is that later countries are larger: total locations actually rise from 13,318 to 19,535 while sweep counts fall from 462 to 318, and the two roughly cancel.

